          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function"} zeta function
            naturally associated to a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+curve"} complex curve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function+of+an+elliptic+differential+operator"} zeta function of an elliptic differential operator
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laplace+operator"} Laplace operator
            on the Riemann surface (and hence essentially the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+fields"} scalar fields
            on that surface) is directly analogous to the zeta functions associated with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+curves"} arithmetic curves
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+L-functions"} Artin L-functions
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#MinakshisundaramPleijel49"}> Minakshisundaram-Pleijel 49
            ) considered the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function+of+an+elliptic+differential+operator"} zeta function of an elliptic differential operator
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laplace+operator"} Laplace operator
            on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
          %p
            Motivated by the resemblance of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Selberg+trace+formula"} Selberg trace formula
            to Weil’s formula for the sum of zeros of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+zeta+function"} Riemann zeta function
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Selberg56"} Selberg 56
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
            a
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function"} zeta function
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Selberg+zeta+function"} Selberg zeta function
                of a Riemann surface
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Bump"} Bump, below theorem 19
          %p
            Much of this is more generally defined/considered on higher dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperbolic+manifolds"} hyperbolic manifolds
          %p
            That the Selberg zeta function is indeed proportional to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function+of+an+elliptic+differential+operator"} zeta function
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laplace+operator"} Laplace operator
            is due to (
            %a{:href => "#DHokerPhong86"}> D’Hoker-Phong 86
            ,
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Sarnak87"} Sarnak 87
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta+function+of+a+self-adjoint+operator"} eta function
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            on the given Riemann surface/hyperbolic manifold goes back to (
            %a{:href => "#Milson78"}> Milson 78
            ), with further development including (
            %a{:href => "#Park01"}> Park 01
            ). For review of the literature on this relation see also the beginning of (
            %a{:href => "#Friedman06"}> Friedman 06
            ).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#for_a_complex_torus__complex_elliptic_curve For a complex torus / complex elliptic curve
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi τ
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}/(\mathbb{Z}\oplus \tau \mathbb{Z})
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+torus"} complex torus
            (complex
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zeta+function+of+an+elliptic+differential+operator"} zeta function
            of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laplace+operator"} Laplace operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ζ
                    %mi Δ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 2
                      %mi s
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 2
                      %mi s
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo ,
                      %mi l
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi k
                          %mo +
                          %mi τ
                          %mi l
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi s
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\zeta_{\Delta} = (2\pi)^{-2 s} E(s)
                  \\coloneqq
                  (2\pi)^{-2 s} \underset{(k,l)\in \mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}-(0,0)}{\sum}
                  \\frac{1}{{\vert k +\tau l\vert}^{2s}}
                  \\,.
          %p
            The corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+determinant"} functional determinant
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi E
                    %mi Δ
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Im
                  %mi τ
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi τ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 4
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(
                  E^\prime_{\Delta}(0)
                  )
                  \= (Im \tau)^2 {\vert \eta(\tau)\vert}^4
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+eta+function"} Dedekind eta function
          %p
            (recalled e.g. in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Todorov03"} Todorov 03, page 3
          %h3#OfDiracOperatorTwistedByFlatConnection Of Dirac operators twisted by a flat connection
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat connection
            on a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_A
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            twisted by this connection.
          %p
            On a suitable
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperbolic+manifold"} hyperbolic manifold
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theta+function"} theta function
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_A
            appears in (
            %a{:href => "#BunkeOlbrich94"}> Bunke-Olbrich 94, prop. 6.3
            ) (and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BunkeOlbrich94a"} Bunke-Olbrich 94a, def. 3.1
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BunkeOlbrich94a"} Bunke-Olbrich 94a, def. 4.1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+L-functions"} Artin L-functions
            with the flat connection replaced by a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+representation"} Galois representation
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#AnalogyWithArtinLFunction Analogy with Artin L-function
          %p
            That the Selberg/Ruelle zeta function is equivalently an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler+product"} Euler product
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+polynomials"} characteristic polynomials
            is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Gangolli77"} Gangolli 77, (2.72)
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Fried86"} Fried 86, prop. 5
          %p
            That it is in particular the Euler product of characteristic polynomials of the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monodromies"} monodromies
